|> Section
    title=The geometric distribution

    We say that $X$ has the geometric distribution with
    parameter $0<p<1$, and denote
    $X\sim \operatorname{Geom}(p)$, if $X$ satisfies

    $$
    \mathrm{P}(X=k) = p(1-p)^{k-1} \qquad (k \ge 1).
    $$

    Some authors prefer a slightly different convention
    whereby the geometric random variables take
    nonnegative values (including $0$) rather than only
    positive values. Thus, denote
    $X' \sim \operatorname{Geom_0}(p)$, and say that $X'$
    has the geometric distribution starting from $0$, if
    it satisfies

    $$
    \mathrm{P}(X'=k) = p(1-p)^{k} \qquad (k \ge ).
    $$

    $\textbf{Properties:}$

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        |> li
            $\operatorname{Geom}(p) \stackrel{d}{=}
            \operatorname{Geom_0}(p) + 1$.

        |> li
            If $W_1,W_2,W_3, \ldots$ is a sequence of i.i.d.
            r.v.'s with distribution $\operatorname{Ber}(p)$,
            then

            $$
            X = \min\{ k\ge 1\,:\, W_k = 1\}  \sim \operatorname{Geom}(p).
            $$

            That is, the distribution $\operatorname{Geom}(p)$
            models the number of identical independent experiments
            we had to perform to get the first successful outcome,
            when each experiment has probability $p$ of success.
            The variant $\operatorname{Geom_0}(p)$ corresponds to
            the number of _failed_ experiments before the first
            success.

        |> li
            The geometric distribution has the (discrete)
            $\textbf{lack of memory property}$. More precisely,
            if $X \sim \operatorname{Geom}(p)$ then

            $$
            \mathrm{P}(X \ge n+k \ |\ X \ge k) = \mathrm{P}(X \ge n)\qquad \textrm{ for all $n,k\ge 1$}.
            $$

        |> li
            If $X\sim \operatorname{Geom}(p)$ then
            $\mathbf{E} X = \frac1p$,
            $\mathbf{V}(X) = \frac{1-p}{p^2}$.

        |> li
            If $X'\sim \operatorname{Geom}(p)$ then
            $\mathbf{E} X' = \frac{1-p}{p}$,
            $\mathbf{V}(X') = \frac{1-p}{p^2}$.